  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}sin\u2061(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-10.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-10.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-10,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-10.\n$$",
